WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.090
Hello, and welcome to Emma Barnett Meets

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:05.940
and this week I'm meeting the space expert

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.610
and physicist, Professor Brian Cox

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.590
and I'm really looking
forward to asking him

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:14.250
about how he gets his head
around the vastness of space

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:17.040
and to explain it to all
of us or try to, anyway.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.930
How he also goes about his daily life

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.870
when he knows how
insignificant we really are

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:24.600
and what he makes of NASA's new telescope

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.033
and those amazing images.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:42.210
Welcome to the program.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.220
Thank you so much for coming to talk to me

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.660
and to all of our viewers.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.690
And I actually wanted to start
with the new NASA telescope.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:50.820
We'll come to your particular
project in a moment,

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.780
but it's named after the man
who ran the agency in the '60s

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.780
and oversaw the first man
mission to the moon, James Webb.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.420
What do you make of what
we're seeing so far?

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:01.830
'Cause it's pretty incredible.

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:03.240
Yeah, it's a remarkable instrument.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:04.740
I mean, you think of the predecessor

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.385
which is Hubble Space telescope,

00:01:06.385 --> 00:01:07.802
I think it's almost impossible

00:01:07.802 --> 00:01:10.163
to imagine a world without that

00:01:10.163 --> 00:01:13.530
even if you don't know the images

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:15.120
that we're familiar with of the universe,

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.640
many of them, the spectacular galaxies

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.430
and star-forming regions,
they're from Hubble.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:23.370
But the Webb is a
significant step forward.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:26.420
And technically, one of
the most important things

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:29.310
is it can see what we call
longer wavelength light

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:30.810
or infrared light.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.120
And that's important
because if you think about,

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.670
we wanna see the first galaxies forming.

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.800
So we want to understand
how the first stars

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.020
and galaxies formed in the universe.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.080
And so what you do is you
look far out into the universe

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.930
and because light travels quite slowly

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.640
across cosmic distances,

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:48.810
let's say you have a galaxy

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:52.110
that's the most distant one
you can see with the naked eye

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:54.300
is about 2 million light years away.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.370
So that means that the
light has been traveling

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:58.320
for 2 million years to reach us.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.050
And it's a remarkable thing
actually to think when you look,

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:02.267
it's called the Andromeda galaxy

00:02:02.267 --> 00:02:03.690
and if you know where you're looking,

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.210
you can just catch it out
of the corner of your eye.

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.370
If you think about it,
you're seeing that as it was

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:10.170
2 million years ago.

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:11.010
Yes, although I don't-
Because it takes

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:12.510
the light 2 million years to travel.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:14.190
I don't like being late to information,

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:16.134
so that makes me feel very out of date.

00:02:16.134 --> 00:02:17.100
It's remarkable.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:21.660
I mean, it began its journey
before we had evolved on earth.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.040
So in the time it took,

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.967
and that's the nearest neighboring galaxy.

00:02:24.967 --> 00:02:29.070
The Webb looks so far out
that it's capturing light

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:32.512
that's been traveling for
over 13 billion years.

00:02:32.512 --> 00:02:34.950
But the universe has been expanding,

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:36.990
and so the light has been stretching.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.360
And so for the most distant galaxies,

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.029
we're looking back in time
almost to the Big Bang.

00:02:43.029 --> 00:02:45.930
The Hubble was not
sensitive to that light,

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:49.410
so that the Webb can see the formation

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:50.490
of the first galaxies.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.650
It's essentially looking all the way back

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:54.720
to very close to the beginning of time.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:55.980
And that's very important

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.200
because we're not entirely sure

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.930
exactly how those first galaxies formed.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.120
The idea of what you just talked about

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.040
in terms of how much time this has taken

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:06.300
and what it's covering,

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:09.000
do you think we will ever
be able to see the Big Bang?

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.330
Well, we almost can.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:14.580
It's not the Webb telescope,
there's something called

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:17.490
the cosmic microwave background radiation,

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:20.850
which is light that was
emitted in the universe

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.350
and it's a very precise number.

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:25.710
It's 380,000 years after the Big Bang

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:27.420
which is not very long at all, right.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:30.090
The universe is 13.8 billion years old.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:31.620
So we can see that,

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.740
it's microwaves because
it's been stretched so much

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:36.540
by the expansion of the universe.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.810
Going back, the problem with light is that

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.130
in those earliest times,

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:43.560
the universe was so hot and so dense

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.480
that light couldn't travel through it.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:46.860
So it was opaque.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:51.270
So you can't use light to
go back earlier than that.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.970
But what we can potentially do, not yet,

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:55.350
we're not good enough yet,

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:57.690
but the technology we use now

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:00.330
to detect colliding black holes,

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:01.633
which is another remarkable thing,

00:04:01.633 --> 00:04:04.230
but I'll leave that one aside for now,

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:06.300
but we have the technology to do that.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.910
And that technology possibly

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:12.870
could allow us to probe
right back to this thing,

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.040
the Big Bang

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:16.140
and again, whether or not
that's the origin of time

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:17.820
we can talk about as well.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:19.050
Brian, you've dedicated your life

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:21.690
to explaining the scale
of the universe to people.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:25.260
You have a new world tour
and show called Horizons.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:26.200
What was the goal?

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.095
Are you trying to teach people new things

00:04:28.095 --> 00:04:30.600
and understand the world
they're living in better?

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.130
Or what are you aiming for?

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:33.840
Well, actually all those things.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.990
So it works, I hope, on different levels.

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:40.740
So I talk about cosmology,
we've spoken about

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.110
what we call the large scale
structure of the universe.

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:45.120
So the galaxies and how they formed,

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:48.000
how the universe has
evolved since the Big Bang.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.310
But also I've got very interested

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:53.100
in my academic work in black holes.

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:55.530
And black holes, and they're
really evocative things.

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:57.750
I think everyone's heard
of these strange things,

00:04:57.750 --> 00:04:59.599
these totally collapsed stars,

00:04:59.599 --> 00:05:02.760
from which nothing apparently can escape.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:06.020
But in the past few years,
past few decades, really,

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:09.150
beginning work that Stephen
Hawking really began

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:11.790
back in the 1970s and many others,

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:14.340
we've begun to suspect
there's a lot more to them.

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:17.310
And they've started forcing us

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:21.420
to reassess our understanding
of what space and time are.

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:23.010
And that's a really weird sentence.

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:25.800
You might think, well, space
is the arena in which we live

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:27.990
and time just ticks, but it really isn't.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:31.260
It looks like, from studying these things,

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:33.150
there are building blocks of space

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:35.550
and building blocks of time.

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:39.150
And what I say in the show is
that's a mind blowing idea,

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:40.500
and I talk about that.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:43.020
But the key point is there's
a great quote from Einstein,

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:45.780
a beautiful story that Einstein told

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:47.010
about when he was a little boy,

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:48.990
he was six or seven years old

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:51.150
and his dad gave him a compass.

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:52.800
And so he looks at the compass

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.900
and he saw, well, there's
this thing, this needle

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:58.320
and it always points north,
points in this direction.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:01.170
So there's something
invisible that I can't see

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:03.330
that underlies our reality,

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:05.910
that's making this needle point north.

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:07.560
And he said later in life,

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:10.530
it was my first encounter with an idea,

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:12.930
which is if you look at nature carefully

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:15.720
and really pay attention and you're lucky,

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:18.593
you can catch a glimpse of
something deeply hidden,

00:06:18.593 --> 00:06:20.370
which is a beautiful phrase.

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:22.200
Something deeply hidden,

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:24.270
which is the deep structure of reality.

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:25.980
It's what our reality is.

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:29.160
And so black holes, they're
kind of a metaphor in a way.

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:32.370
I talk about them as
Rosetta stones in the sky.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:34.860
They're the things that by studying them,

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:37.740
and you say, "Why, why would
we study these things?"

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:39.000
Well in studying these things,

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:41.970
we're beginning to get a deep picture

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:44.160
of what our reality actually is.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.890
And that's a remarkable idea

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:49.440
but it's a beautiful idea that
runs through all of science.

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.600
How do you define a black hole?

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:53.757
Well, it's in Einstein's theory,

00:06:53.757 --> 00:06:55.650
which is again it's a remarkable thing,

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:58.710
it's probably in 1915,
over a hundred years old.

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:01.170
But it's just a region of space

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:04.290
from which even light can't escape.

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.390
And by understanding what they are,

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:10.590
and knowing more about
them, what do we then learn?

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:13.350
Well, so Stephen Hawking back in the '70s

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:14.626
published a paper,

00:07:14.626 --> 00:07:17.820
the initial one was called
"Black Hole Explosions",

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:19.860
which is a great title for a paper.

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:24.090
And he calculated, he
found out that black holes,

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:27.930
in his language, he said
black holes ain't so black.

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:32.010
They glow in the sky like coals in the sky

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:33.210
and they radiate.

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:35.910
And so over time, they lose energy

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:40.320
and mass and ultimately
disappear over huge time scales.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:44.460
And that's so important and I
show this picture in the show

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:46.470
that if you go into Westminster Abbey

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:48.660
and look on the floor
of Westminster Abbey,

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:52.800
on Stephen's memorial stone,
then you'd find his equation

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:54.570
for the temperature of a black hole

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:58.650
literally chiseled in stone
on the floor of an abbey.

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:00.300
So you might say, "Why?

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:02.220
Why is it so important?"

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:05.400
This was the key, this was
his Rosetta stone idea.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:08.280
In trying to understand what happens,

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:11.338
what happens to the stuff that fell in?

00:08:11.338 --> 00:08:14.250
When you thought these
things existed forever,

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:16.436
which is pre-Hawking, right,

00:08:16.436 --> 00:08:17.820
then you think, well, it's okay.

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:19.710
It gets locked up inside,
it can never get out.

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:20.940
We don't care.

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:25.020
But the thing evaporates
away, one day it will be gone.

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:27.300
So then suddenly you have to
be faced with this question,

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:28.905
what happened to everything then?

00:08:28.905 --> 00:08:31.740
If I threw a book into a black hole,

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:34.620
is it somehow possible in the far future

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:37.567
if you collect all this
so-called Hawking radiation

00:08:37.567 --> 00:08:40.560
that comes off, is it
possible to reconstruct

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:42.300
the information in the book?

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:45.480
And that's been a
question, simple question,

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:48.900
that's driven this
tremendous amount of research

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:50.640
for 50 years

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:54.733
and it was pretty much sold
in 2019, actually, in 2020,

00:08:54.733 --> 00:08:55.800
pretty much.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:58.050
I mean, there's still a
huge number of questions.

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:00.930
But roughly, well, the statement is

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:03.720
everything comes out again,
all the information comes out.

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:06.710
So everything that fell in, in principle,

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:10.290
in the far future you could
reconstruct the information

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:11.640
of everything that fell in.

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:13.890
It just an astonishing idea

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:15.870
because actually, the last
thing I'll say is that

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:18.480
before that, before the Hawking papers

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:21.000
and before this modern understanding,

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:24.270
inside a black hole, just
according to Einstein,

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:26.340
sits the end of time

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:28.440
which is an astonishing thing to say,

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:29.610
'cause we can see them.

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:31.920
We have a photograph
of one of these things

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:34.650
in the center of galaxy too, actually.

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:36.313
And when you look at that photograph,

00:09:36.313 --> 00:09:40.015
you're looking at the
end of time in space.

00:09:40.015 --> 00:09:42.570
So then you think, well, if
things go to the end of time,

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:44.070
how does everything get out again?

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:47.100
And that's the content
of this tremendous work

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:48.250
in theoretical physics.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:52.542
This work, studying black holes,

00:09:52.542 --> 00:09:53.375
it's one of those things

00:09:53.375 --> 00:09:55.920
that a certain kind of person
says is useless, right.

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:56.880
He said, "Why?

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:58.170
Who cares?"

00:09:58.170 --> 00:10:01.620
It turns out that the techniques
that are being developed

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:03.690
and the understanding that's being gained

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:05.310
from looking at these things

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:07.260
has got a very strong crossover

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:09.693
with building quantum computers.

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:14.070
Quantum computers are in laboratories now,

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:15.930
they have a tremendous potential

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:18.150
to revolutionize our civilization.

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:20.670
Incredibly powerful computing devices.

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:23.100
Well yes, and also that
sometimes the defense given,

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:26.130
isn't it, about what's going into

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:29.010
man going back to the moon or
woman going back to the moon.

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:31.470
The other things are
discovered in the pursuit

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:32.760
of those missions.

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:36.090
Yeah, I mean, it's always
a cliche, but it's true.

00:10:36.090 --> 00:10:38.587
A friend of mine, an
astronomer, always says,

00:10:38.587 --> 00:10:40.590
"When people criticize these spacecraft

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:42.570
and they say you know, the Webb,

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:44.820
let's say the James Webb
space telescope was what,

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:47.310
6 billion or so."

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:49.350
But it's appropriate to point out

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:51.780
that nobody put 6 billion in a suitcase

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:53.730
and launched it on the rockets.

00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:56.190
The 6 billion didn't go into space.

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:58.620
It was spent on earth.

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:00.630
And what is often the case,

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:03.990
so putting aside the glory and wonder

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:06.300
of the discoveries that are made,

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:11.250
that basically, you are paying
people to do high tech jobs

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:14.220
and do research and build
machines that operate

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:16.830
at the edge of the possible

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:21.780
and history tells us that that
tends to be extremely useful.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:24.870
You're inventing things
and trying to do things

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:25.800
that are difficult

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:29.130
and that expertise never
stays in one place.

00:11:29.130 --> 00:11:31.650
It then spreads out
across our civilization

00:11:31.650 --> 00:11:34.320
in ways that you can't really
predict or even quantify.

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:36.960
Indeed and you did sound
very excited earlier.

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:38.130
I mean, you sound excited the whole time

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:40.380
about all of this, which is infectious

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:43.800
even if we're, people trying
to keep up, including myself.

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:46.680
But when you talked about
the golden possibility

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:49.377
of there being life out there,

00:11:49.377 --> 00:11:52.230
and that question of are we alone?

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:53.670
Where are you with that?

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:55.830
What do you say to people right now

00:11:55.830 --> 00:11:58.080
with the latest information?

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:03.080
Well, so again, I think
there's in cosmology,

00:12:03.487 --> 00:12:06.300
I say this in the show as well,

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:08.160
it's one of the most challenging subjects

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:11.670
because at one level, it
makes us feel very, very small

00:12:11.670 --> 00:12:14.250
and insignificant and
it's true, physically.

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:16.350
I mean the earth is one planet

00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:20.280
around one star amongst 400
billion stars in one galaxy

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:22.290
amongst 2 trillion galaxies

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:24.510
in the part of the universe we can see.

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:25.658
So we're definitely small, right?

00:12:25.658 --> 00:12:26.970
So you can't argue that.

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:28.980
Yes, you've clarified that.

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:30.450
But, you're right.

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:32.406
If you ask the question about life,

00:12:32.406 --> 00:12:33.960
the answer is we don't know.

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:37.230
We found nothing, nothing
alive beyond the earth.

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:39.180
But admittedly, we haven't been very far

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:40.950
or looked tremendously hard.

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:43.260
Although we are looking hard now on Mars

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:47.550
because we suspect there might
have been microbes on Mars

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:49.380
because there was once water on Mars

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:51.210
and it was geologically active

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:52.800
and all the things that we think

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:56.130
led to the origin of life on
earth were present on Mars

00:12:56.130 --> 00:12:57.390
around the same time,

00:12:57.390 --> 00:12:59.910
about three and a half
to 4 billion years ago.

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:01.350
So we wouldn't be surprised

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:03.330
if we find that microbes existed,

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:05.718
probably, subsurface on Mars

00:13:05.718 --> 00:13:08.640
or maybe on some of the
moons of Jupiter or Saturn

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:10.710
where there's water today.

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:12.360
However,

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:16.050
nobody expects anything more
complicated than a microbe.

00:13:16.050 --> 00:13:17.312
And it's interesting.

00:13:17.312 --> 00:13:20.790
So, if you look at the
history of life on earth,

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:24.450
you have microbes around
3.8 billion years ago,

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:26.550
pretty much, not long
after the earth formed

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:27.963
in geological time.

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:33.030
But if you look for evidence
of complex life on earth,

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:35.850
then really there isn't
any in the fossil record

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:39.150
until about 600 million years ago or so.

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:43.590
So on this planet, for most
of the history of the planet,

00:13:43.590 --> 00:13:45.870
there was slime, basically.

00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:49.230
Single-celled things
doing interesting stuff,

00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:50.970
photosynthesis and things,

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:54.150
but nothing more complicated
than a single cell.

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:56.400
And it's only in the last
half a billion years or so

00:13:56.400 --> 00:13:58.860
that you get this
explosion of life on earth.

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:02.040
And only as I said, it's only in the last

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:03.420
less than a million years

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:05.670
that we've had Homo sapiens on earth.

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:07.950
And in the last few tens
of thousands of years,

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:09.570
we've had a civilization.

00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:14.280
So here, it took pretty
much 4 billion years

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:16.350
to go from cell to civilization.

00:14:16.350 --> 00:14:18.930
And that's a third of
the age of the universe.

00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:22.283
And so that leads many
biologists that I speak to

00:14:22.283 --> 00:14:25.110
to suggest that whilst
microbes might be common,

00:14:25.110 --> 00:14:27.150
civilizations might be rare.

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:28.260
Actually I asked a friend of mine,

00:14:28.260 --> 00:14:30.600
a great physicist, Sean Carroll,

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:33.030
how many civilizations do you think

00:14:33.030 --> 00:14:35.880
there will be in a Milky Way
galaxy, a typical galaxy?

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:37.590
How many in a typical galaxy?

00:14:37.590 --> 00:14:39.207
And he said, "None."

00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:41.340
Right, none.

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:43.952
And I tend to say one.

00:14:43.952 --> 00:14:47.250
So somewhere between
zero and one, probably.

00:14:47.250 --> 00:14:48.750
But, that's a guess.

00:14:48.750 --> 00:14:51.090
But do you want there to be?
I think it's a good guess.

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:52.590
Brian, is that what it is?

00:14:52.590 --> 00:14:55.110
You sound like perhaps you
have an optimism for it,

00:14:55.110 --> 00:14:57.370
and I wonder what's underpinning that?

00:14:57.370 --> 00:14:59.490
I would love there to be.

00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:01.980
And actually, because it worries me

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:04.770
and this is the way that
I end my show, actually.

00:15:04.770 --> 00:15:08.100
It worries me that let's
take that position.

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:13.100
Let's imagine that in our
galaxy, 400 billion suns,

00:15:13.830 --> 00:15:16.380
there's just us that thinks, right.

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:18.330
I mean microbes all over the place,

00:15:18.330 --> 00:15:21.960
but in terms of things
that think and can feel,

00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:22.976
and in a very real sense,

00:15:22.976 --> 00:15:25.380
bring meaning to the universe,

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:26.550
all these things we've talked about,

00:15:26.550 --> 00:15:28.380
the beauty of these galaxies.

00:15:28.380 --> 00:15:29.280
They're not beautiful

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:31.230
if there's nothing there
to perceive them, right?

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:32.820
They're just galaxies.

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:35.700
So if that's it in this galaxy,

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:38.560
then the decisions that we make now,

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:43.560
as a civilization, have
a, galactic implications.

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:45.780
If we destroy ourselves, for example,

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:47.820
deliberately or through inaction,

00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:51.480
then it's possible that we
eliminate meaning perhaps forever

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:53.880
in a galaxy of 400 billion stars.

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:57.210
So I think that leads you to,

00:15:57.210 --> 00:15:58.650
it's a good working assumption,

00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:01.980
that we have a tremendous
responsibility, in a sense,

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:03.450
not to do that.

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:06.180
So I would be much more comfortable

00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:07.702
with our current predicament

00:16:07.702 --> 00:16:11.550
if the galaxy was filled
with civilizations.

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:13.590
Then I'd say, "Well, okay.

00:16:13.590 --> 00:16:15.300
It would be rather stupid of us

00:16:15.300 --> 00:16:18.870
to eliminate all this
beautiful culture and science

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:20.790
and arts and music that we built up.

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:23.190
But at least there's someone
else that's doing that."

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:25.560
But I'm not actually sure there is.

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:28.650
How comfortable are you with space tourism

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:31.680
and the pursuit of that
knowledge and meaning

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:35.880
being left largely to a very
small group of rich men?

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:36.713
You're right.

00:16:36.713 --> 00:16:40.206
The idea that the technology is used,

00:16:40.206 --> 00:16:43.110
if all it was used for was to send a few

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:45.870
very, very rich people
into space on a joy ride,

00:16:45.870 --> 00:16:48.900
then there will be a real criticism there.

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:51.390
But that's not all the
technology is used for.

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:55.710
So I think it's important
to understand that

00:16:55.710 --> 00:17:00.710
the space industry is
about operating initially,

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:04.055
near earth orbits and we've
already industrialized that.

00:17:04.055 --> 00:17:06.060
We all use it everyday.

00:17:06.060 --> 00:17:08.640
I mean, we all switch on
our sat navs in the car,

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:13.640
satellite navigation, it
requires infrastructure in orbit.

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:15.855
A lot of the stuff we
know about climate change,

00:17:15.855 --> 00:17:18.930
resource use and so on,
that's coming from satellites.

00:17:18.930 --> 00:17:23.930
So I think you have to look at
the space industry as a whole

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:27.112
really and say, well, it's not just about,

00:17:27.112 --> 00:17:29.605
the same argument was made

00:17:29.605 --> 00:17:33.510
for intercontinental travel by aircraft,

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:35.730
it's true, when the first
aircraft were built.

00:17:35.730 --> 00:17:38.610
It was extremely expensive
in the preserve of the rich.

00:17:38.610 --> 00:17:40.470
But of course, within a few decades

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:42.480
it had democratized travel

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:45.780
and we all take advantage
of that every day.

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:47.970
So I think that's the
right way to look at space.

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:51.210
It's not to focus on particular bits of it

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:55.100
that we might not like for
moral reasons or whatever it is.

00:17:55.100 --> 00:17:57.870
So it's actually, I really do advocate

00:17:57.870 --> 00:18:00.120
for the fact of looking
at the whole industry,

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:03.570
which is tremendously important now.

00:18:03.570 --> 00:18:07.380
It is, I mean I am interested
though that you did say,

00:18:07.380 --> 00:18:08.730
and correct me if I'm wrong,

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:11.880
that all political leaders
should go to space,

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:13.599
perhaps for some perspective.

00:18:13.599 --> 00:18:15.683
Do you still think that?
Yeah, and I did say oh,

00:18:15.683 --> 00:18:17.553
most of them should come back.

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:20.273
Most of them.

00:18:20.273 --> 00:18:21.450
Very good, very good,

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:23.279
that's the last part of that quote.

00:18:23.279 --> 00:18:24.810
Yeah.

00:18:24.810 --> 00:18:28.860
Yeah, it was based on an idea,

00:18:28.860 --> 00:18:31.490
which is a very famous idea
called the overview effect,

00:18:31.490 --> 00:18:36.490
which is well known that
astronauts change their view

00:18:36.870 --> 00:18:41.040
of the always, as far as I'm aware anyway,

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:42.780
that when they come back,

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:45.000
they have a more positive view

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:48.120
of the value of this planet

00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:51.210
and are very much more
focused on preserving it

00:18:51.210 --> 00:18:52.350
as a beautiful place,

00:18:52.350 --> 00:18:54.961
perhaps a unique place in
a galaxy, as I've said.

00:18:54.961 --> 00:18:57.090
So I felt that,

00:18:57.090 --> 00:19:01.530
and I do feel that having the good fortune

00:19:01.530 --> 00:19:03.330
speaking to many astronauts,

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:04.863
that their experience,

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:08.460
if the people who make the decisions,

00:19:08.460 --> 00:19:10.590
the big decisions about our future,

00:19:10.590 --> 00:19:11.820
could have that experience,

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:13.320
I think would be good investments.

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:16.051
It's extremely hard argument to make

00:19:16.051 --> 00:19:20.640
that we should actually spend
money allowing, you know,

00:19:20.640 --> 00:19:23.790
in our case, at the moment,
our Prime Minister, right.

00:19:23.790 --> 00:19:24.937
So in the UK, we'd say,

00:19:24.937 --> 00:19:27.079
"Well, we should spend
money sending Boris Johnson

00:19:27.079 --> 00:19:29.970
into space and bringing him
back safely to earth again

00:19:29.970 --> 00:19:31.560
because it would change his view."

00:19:31.560 --> 00:19:33.560
Now I don't think I'd win that argument.

00:19:34.500 --> 00:19:36.390
No, but-
But,

00:19:36.390 --> 00:19:38.580
I think there's a serious point there,

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:43.410
which is that it does seem to
change people for the better

00:19:43.410 --> 00:19:45.113
when they see our earth as it really is,

00:19:45.113 --> 00:19:48.510
which is a fragile thing
against the blackness of space.

00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:52.020
So I think it is an argument
that I would try to make.

00:19:52.020 --> 00:19:53.850
I would lose it

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:55.573
because it's very expensive.

00:19:55.573 --> 00:19:58.440
Do you want to go, are you willing to go?

00:19:58.440 --> 00:20:00.993
I would love to, because as I said,

00:20:01.890 --> 00:20:04.968
I'd love to share that experience

00:20:04.968 --> 00:20:07.020
of seeing our world from space.

00:20:07.020 --> 00:20:09.480
I think it's really, unarguably,

00:20:09.480 --> 00:20:12.480
it appears to be a life
changing experience

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:14.940
and it seems to change
individuals for the better.

00:20:14.940 --> 00:20:16.290
You won't be scared?

00:20:16.290 --> 00:20:17.430
Well, I don't know.

00:20:17.430 --> 00:20:21.360
I mean, I remember one of
my great heroes, John Young,

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:24.570
who flew on the Apollo
missions, walked on the moon,

00:20:24.570 --> 00:20:26.730
test flew the first space shuttle

00:20:26.730 --> 00:20:29.550
and he said that anyone who sits on top of

00:20:29.550 --> 00:20:34.440
however many thousands of tons
of liquid hydrogen and oxygen

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:37.240
and isn't a bit worried,
doesn't understand the problem.

00:20:39.240 --> 00:20:40.593
So he's probably right.

00:20:41.670 --> 00:20:44.880
Do you have people come and
talk to you about faith?

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:46.937
Do they talk to you about how religion

00:20:46.937 --> 00:20:51.330
and that side of life fits
into your level of knowledge?

00:20:51.330 --> 00:20:52.709
Because I know you've
traveled around the world

00:20:52.709 --> 00:20:54.840
talking to people.

00:20:54.840 --> 00:20:55.673
Yeah, a lot.

00:20:55.673 --> 00:20:58.205
It comes up as a question

00:20:58.205 --> 00:21:03.205
because cosmology it's
a science of origins.

00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:06.240
And actually, with the
study of black holes,

00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:07.462
as I've mentioned,
we're even beginning now

00:21:07.462 --> 00:21:10.410
to think about what space and time are.

00:21:10.410 --> 00:21:13.980
So naturally the, I say
it in the show, actually,

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:15.420
I say, when we're talking about this,

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:18.330
it's the moment of creation if there was.

00:21:18.330 --> 00:21:21.270
So we're asking, was there
a moment of creation?

00:21:21.270 --> 00:21:23.571
And that's the correct
language to use, I think.

00:21:23.571 --> 00:21:25.350
It's evocative language,

00:21:25.350 --> 00:21:27.060
but it's really what we're talking about.

00:21:27.060 --> 00:21:30.180
Did everything come into being,
or has it always been here?

00:21:30.180 --> 00:21:31.500
We actually don't even know

00:21:31.500 --> 00:21:33.300
the answer to that question, really.

00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:35.700
So yeah, I get asked it a lot

00:21:35.700 --> 00:21:39.000
because I think cosmology

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:41.250
is a challenging science

00:21:41.250 --> 00:21:43.710
that challenges us to
think about these things

00:21:43.710 --> 00:21:44.880
that we often, you know,

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:48.123
we associate that with
theology or philosophy.

00:21:48.990 --> 00:21:51.059
And I think that's a good thing

00:21:51.059 --> 00:21:54.810
because something I
very strongly believe in

00:21:54.810 --> 00:21:59.400
is that segmenting or
making little canisters

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:02.437
and putting different
bits of human experience

00:22:02.437 --> 00:22:05.580
or different music and art and theology

00:22:05.580 --> 00:22:08.190
and philosophy and science
and segmenting them all

00:22:08.190 --> 00:22:10.126
is just the wrong way to look at it.

00:22:10.126 --> 00:22:15.126
What we are doing is a deep
level trying to understand

00:22:15.990 --> 00:22:19.230
what it means to be
human in this tremendous,

00:22:19.230 --> 00:22:21.240
potentially, infinite universe.

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:25.440
And so those questions get asked.

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:29.160
And ultimately I say, I'm
quite straightforward,

00:22:29.160 --> 00:22:32.645
I say, "I'm not a
religious person myself."

00:22:32.645 --> 00:22:35.610
But the correct thing to say,

00:22:35.610 --> 00:22:37.680
even about the question did the universe

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:41.250
have a beginning in
time, is we don't know.

00:22:41.250 --> 00:22:43.000
We don't know and that's it

00:22:44.010 --> 00:22:45.780
Professor Brian Cox, thank you so much

00:22:45.780 --> 00:22:47.489
for your time and your insight.

00:22:47.489 --> 00:22:48.750
Thank you very much.

00:22:48.750 --> 00:22:51.270
And thank you to you for being with us.

00:22:51.270 --> 00:22:52.893
Until next time, goodbye.

